Theorem TF-N-A: logical ownership effects are destination-free and demand-free

  Preconditions:
    - The canonical logical effect set is
      {OwnerCredit, Release, Cleanup}.
    - Logical effects are calculus terms. They do not name a reference
      counter, storage layout, or instruction encoding.
    - The current transitional carrier set is enumerated separately as
      {RcInc, RcDec, BurdenInc, BurdenDec}. Carrier membership establishes
      coverage only; it does not promote those names into the calculus.
    - Burden-op elimination soundness remains the §11 CH-1/CH-2 obligation.
      TF-N/A classifies only the transfer shape.

  Soundness property:
    Forall event in {OwnerCredit, Release, Cleanup}.
      transfer_tf_n_a(event) = NoDst
      AND backward_demand_tf_n_a(event) = empty
      AND L-6 layer (b) monotonicity holds vacuously.

    Forall carrier in {RcInc, RcDec, BurdenInc, BurdenDec}.
      refines_tf_n_a_shape(carrier)
      AND transfer_tf_n_a(carrier) = NoDst
      AND backward_demand_tf_n_a(carrier) = empty.

  Proof obligation:
    Four-part discharge:
      (a) Logical classification — enumerate OwnerCredit, Release, Cleanup and
          prove each has no SSA destination and creates no TF-11 demand.
      (b) Transitional coverage — enumerate RcInc, RcDec, BurdenInc, BurdenDec
          and prove each refines the same no-destination/no-demand shape.
      (c) Separation — assert the logical event names and transitional carrier
          names are disjoint sets. Changing a carrier cannot change TF-N/A.
      (d) Coverage gate — exactly 3 logical rows + 4 carrier-refinement rows =
          7 checked cases; no row may be dropped.

    Logical-event semantics:
      OwnerCredit records one additional logical owner.
      Release discharges one logical owner credit.
      Cleanup records terminal discharge and cleanup obligations.
      None defines a value, and none is an operand use for backward demand.

    Transitional-carrier interpretation:
      RcInc / RcDec and BurdenInc / BurdenDec are current MIR spellings that
      project or defer the logical events. Appendix A's side-effect-only rows
      carry "—" across every dimension. Physical counter motion and
      reclamation remain projection concerns.

    Negative-direction witness:
      Treating BurdenInc or BurdenDec as a different logical transfer class, or
      treating RcInc/RcDec as the canonical calculus vocabulary, violates the
      required logical/carrier separation and is rejected.

    Engines dispatched:
      monotonicity (PRIMARY — vacuous L-6 layer (b) discharge)
      case_analysis (PRIMARY — 3 logical effects + 4 transitional carriers)
      lattice (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; no codomain element)
      refinement (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; no destination)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_04 verifies three destination-free logical ownership effects separately from four transitional carrier refinements, with seven-case coverage and no TF-11 demand
